<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Velabri</h1><p class="st-meta">100 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">People of south-west Ireland recorded by Ptolemy, usually placed on the Atlantic coast of Kerry beside the Iverni.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Velabri are known only from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geography-ptolemy">Ptolemy's Geography</a> of about 150, which places them on the south-west coast of Ireland, south of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gangani-ireland">Gangani (Ireland)</a> and west of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/iverni">Iverni</a>, roughly in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-kerry">County Kerry</a>. Ptolemy's information on Ireland came from earlier reports by sailors along the Atlantic coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nothing is recorded of their rulers, settlements or customs. The peninsulas of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ciarraige-luachra">Kerry</a> were a land of stone forts, promontory forts and pastoral farming, and the region later produced many ogham stones, showing that Irish-speaking communities with strong lineage traditions lived there by the 5th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Velabri do not appear in any later source, and no Irish population name has been securely matched with theirs. Their region later formed part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-munster">Kingdom of Munster</a> and the lands of peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corcu-duibne">Corcu Duibne</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ciarraige-luachra">Ciarraige Luachra</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 100 CE – 200 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">British Isles</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:61.69%;width:1.99%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Goidelic Celtic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=150&amp;focus=velabri" aria-label="Velabri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4531 354 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Velabri lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4851 610h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4851 610h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ireland. <a href="/atlas?year=150&amp;focus=velabri">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

